{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations","id":181472,"title":"Massachusetts SWAT teams claim they\u2019re private corporations","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-06-26T17:49:38-07:00","id":181472,"title":"Massachusetts SWAT teams claim they\u2019re private corporations","content":"Figured this need it's own topic outside of my news thread.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-watch/wp/2014/06/26/massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-theyre-private-corporations-immune-from-open-records-laws/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/t...-records-laws/</a> As part of the <a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-watch/wp/2014/06/24/new-aclu-report-takes-a-snapshot-of-police-militarization-in-the-united-states/\" target=\"_blank\">American Civil Liberties Union\u2019s recent report on police militarization</a>, the Massachusetts chapter of the organization sent open records requests to SWAT teams across that state. It received an interesting response.<br /> As it turns out, a number of SWAT teams in the Bay State are operated by what are called law enforcement councils, or LECs. These LECs are funded by several police agencies in a given geographic area and overseen by an executive board, which is usually made up of police chiefs from member police departments. In 2012, for example, the Tewksbury Police Department <a href=\"http://www.tewksbury.info/Pages/TewksburyMA_Manager/budgets/FY2012%20Budget/police.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">paid about $4,600</a> in annual membership dues to the <a href=\"http://www.nemlec.com/index.htm\" target=\"_blank\">North Eastern Massachusetts Law Enforcement Council</a>, or NEMLEC. (See page 36 of linked PDF.) That LEC has about 50 member agencies. In addition to operating a regional SWAT team, the LECs also facilitate technology and information sharing and oversee other specialized units, such as crime scene investigators and computer crime specialists.<br /> Some of these LECs have also apparently incorporated as 501(c)(3) organizations. And it\u2019s here that we run into problems. According to the ACLU, the LECs are claiming that the 501(c)(3) status means that they\u2019re private corporations, not government agencies. And therefore, they say they\u2019re immune from open records requests. Let\u2019s be clear. These agencies oversee police activities. They employ cops who carry guns, wear badges, collect paychecks provided by taxpayers and have the power to detain, arrest, injure and kill. They operate SWAT teams, which conduct raids on private residences. And yet they say that because they\u2019ve incorporated, they\u2019re immune to Massachusetts open records laws. The state\u2019s residents aren\u2019t permitted to know how often the SWAT teams are used, what they\u2019re used for, what sort of training they get or who they\u2019re primarily used against.<br /> <b>(Read more: </b><b><a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-watch/wp/2014/06/24/new-aclu-report-takes-a-snapshot-of-police-militarization-in-the-united-states/\" target=\"_blank\">New ACLU report confirms the stunning rise in police militarization</a>)</b><br /> From <a href=\"http://www.privacysos.org/swat#secrecy\" target=\"_blank\">the ACLU of Massachusetts\u2019s report</a> on police militarization in that state:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Approximately 240 of the 351 police departments in Massachusetts belong to an LEC. While set up as \u201ccorporations,\u201d LECs are funded by local and federal taxpayer money, are composed exclusively of public police officers and sheriffs, and carry out traditional law enforcement functions through specialized units such as SWAT teams . . .</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Due to the weakness of <a href=\"http://aclum.org/public_records\" target=\"_blank\">Massachusetts public records law </a>and the culture of secrecy that has infected local police departments and Law Enforcement Councils, procuring empirical records from police departments and regional SWAT teams in Massachusetts about police militarization was universally difficult and, in most instances, impossible . . .</span><br /> Police departments and regional SWAT teams are public institutions, working with public money, meant to protect and serve the public\u2019s interest. If these institutions do not maintain and make public comprehensive and comprehensible documents pertaining to their operations and tactics, the people cannot judge whether officials are acting appropriately or make needed policy changes when problems arise . . .<br /> <br /> Hiding behind the argument that they are private corporations not subject to the public records laws, the LECs have refused to provide documents regarding their SWAT team policies and procedures. They have also failed to disclose anything about their operations, including how many raids they have executed or for what purpose . . .<br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><a href=\"https://twitter.com/MetroLEC\" target=\"_blank\">METROLEC</a>, one of the largest of the law enforcement councils covering the metropolitan Boston area, operates a range of specialized resources, including a Canine Unit, Computer Crimes Unit, Crisis Negotiation Team, Mobile Operations Motorcycle Unit, and Regional Response Team, in addition to its SWAT force. The organization maintains its own BearCat armored vehicle, as well as a $700,000 state of the art command and control post. In 2012, METROLEC reportedly used its BearCat 26 times, <a href=\"http://www.bostonglobe.com/business/2013/01/04/pittsfield-company-selling-bulletproof-security-sparks-debate-over-cost/2DAQ3GHM8b4eNdeXcL2NqJ/story.html\" target=\"_blank\">mostly for drug busts</a>, and applied to the Federal Aviation Administration<a href=\"https://www.privacysos.org/node/854\" target=\"_blank\"> to obtain a drone license.</a></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">The North Eastern Massachusetts Law Enforcement Council (NEMLEC) similarly operates a SWAT team, as well as a Computer Crime Unit, Motorcycle Unit, School Threat Assessment &amp; Response System, and Regional Communications and Incident Management Assistance Team. Its SWAT team members are <a href=\"http://nemlec.com/\" target=\"_blank\">trained and equipped </a>to \u201cdeal with active shooters, armed barricaded subjects, hostage takers and terrorists,\u201d and they dress in military-style gear with the words \u201cNEMLEC SWAT\u201d emblazoned on their uniforms. Given this training, it is not surprising that the NEMLEC SWAT team has over the past decade led numerous operations that involved armored vehicles, <a href=\"http://www.nydailynews.com/news/national/parents-toddler-injured-flash-bang-botched-raid-call-justice-article-1.1825366\" target=\"_blank\">flash-bang devices</a>, and automatic weapons.</span><br /> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\">(Note: In addition to the LEC SWAT teams, the ACLU notes that at least 25 other Massachusetts cities and towns have their own SWAT-like units, along with the state police and the Massachusetts Bay Transit Authority.)</span><br /> Massachusetts also has a long history of accountability and excessive force problems with SWAT teams. A few examples:<ul><li>In 1988, Boston Det. Sherman Griffiths was killed in a botched drug raid later revealed to have been conducted based on information from an informant a subsequent investigation revealed that the police had simply made up.</li> <li>Six years later, the Rev. Accelyne Williams died of a heart attack during a mistaken drug raid on his home. The Boston Globe found that three of the officers involved in that raid had been accused in a 1989 civil rights suit of using fictional informants to obtain warrants for drug raids. In testimony for that suit, one witness testified that after realizing they\u2019d just raided the wrong home, a Boston police officer shrugged, apologized and said, \u201cThis happens all the time.\u201d The city settled with the plaintiffs.</li> <li>In 1996, the Fitchburg SWAT team was already facing a lawsuit for harassing a group of loiterers when it burned down an apartment complex during a botched drug raid. The SWAT team subsequently faced a number of other allegations of recklessness and misconduct.</li> <li>In January 2011, a SWAT team raided the Framingham, Mass., home of 68-year-old Eurie Stamps at around midnight on a drug warrant. Oddly, it had already arrested the subject of the warrant \u2014 Stamps\u2019s 20-year-old stepson \u2014 outside the house. But because he lived in Stamps\u2019s home, the team went ahead with the raid anyway. When the team encountered Stamps, it instructed him to lie on the floor. He complied. According to the police account, as one officer then moved toward Stamps to check for weapons, he lost his balance and fell. As he fell, his weapon discharged, sending a bullet directly into Stamps\u2019s chest, killing him.</li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">\u201cYou can\u2019t have it both ways,\u201d Jessie Rossman, a staff attorney for the Massachusetts ACLU, told me in a phone interview. \u201cThe same government authority that allows them to carry weapons, make arrests, and break down the doors of Massachusetts residents during dangerous raids also makes them a government agency that is subject to the open records law.\u201d</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>(Read more: <a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-watch/wp/2014/06/26/idaho-deputies-confront-handcuff-man-over-nickelback-but-not-because-hes-a-fan/\" target=\"_blank\">Police handcuff man over Nickelback \u2014 but not because he\u2019s a fan</a>)</b></span><br /> In some states, police agencies can claim exemptions from open records legislation for certain types of requests, such as for internal personnel files, or investigation documents that could reveal the identities of witnesses or informants. In some parts of the country, like the Virginia suburbs of Washington, police agencies have broadly interpreted open records laws to allow them <a href=\"http://reason.com/archives/2010/04/05/the-nova-police-blackout\" target=\"_blank\">to turn down just about every request.</a> But this claim in Massachusetts is on a whole different scale.<br /> \u201cThey didn\u2019t even attempt to claim an exception,\u201d Rossman says. \u201cThey\u2019re simply asserting that they\u2019re private corporations.\u201d<br /> <a href=\"http://aclum.org/news_6.24.14a\" target=\"_blank\">The ACLU is now suing NEMLEC</a>. It\u2019s worth noting that in addition to receiving taxpayer funding from its 51 member police agencies, NEMLEC has also received significant federal funding over the years, particularly from the Byrne Grant program. In fact, <a href=\"http://www.eagletribune.com/local/x1445029096/Drug-busts-net-five-suspects/print\" target=\"_blank\">just last April</a>, NEMLEC made a series of drug busts across the state in an investigation funded at least in part with Byrne Grants. (NEMLEC <a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?client=safari&amp;rls=en&amp;q=NEMLEC+%22drug+raid%22&amp;ie=UTF-8&amp;oe=UTF-8\" target=\"_blank\">seems to be involved in a lot of drug raids</a>.) In 2010, NEMLEC <a href=\"http://www.gpo.gov/fdsys/pkg/CRPT-111srpt229/html/CRPT-111srpt229.htm\" target=\"_blank\">received an $800,000 Byrne Grant</a> earmarked by then-Sen. John F. Kerry.<br /> Interestingly, in 2009, <a href=\"http://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/2009/August/09-civ-808.html\" target=\"_blank\">NEMLEC had to pay out $200,000</a> \u201c<span style=\"color:#171e24\">to settle allegations that it made false claims related to the use of Justice Department grant funds\u201d \u2014 specifically, funds obtained through the Byrne Grant program. That sounds like an agency that could use a little oversight.</span><br /> The argument that the LECs in Massachusetts are private corporations and therefore immune to the state open records law was made by Jack Collins, the general counsel for the Massachusetts Chiefs of Police Association. I have contacted his office to request an interview but haven\u2019t yet heard back.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-26T23:38:00-07:00","id":181714,"title":"","content":"This would call for a Hitler card with Uncle Joe Combo but I'll just ask Ipsus quod custodias?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1700-blue-fox","name":"Blue Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T01:17:35-07:00","id":181762,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blue Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=181714#post181714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This would call for a Hitler card with Uncle Joe Combo but I'll just ask Ipsus quod custodias?</div> </div> </div> </div>Really those don't even compare anymore. This is ripped out of a Gibson novel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T06:47:34-07:00","id":181900,"title":"","content":"This is somewhat misleading. Only the records which were held by the LEC were refused under the ACLU's request. The swat policies and procedures executed by the police departments (who actually administer the SWAT teams) were turned over in compliance with the law. The only records refused were those that have to do with administering the tests and qualifications exams, which is what the LEC runs as a not for profit contractor signed on by the local government.<br /> <br /> Really, the local governments just need to have the police departments run those directly.<br /> <br /> It sounds conspiratorial as hell, but it's actually not. (Not to say there aren't other signs of police militarization... most of which is driven by the DOD needing to shed vehicles we were ordered to purchase and couldn't use outside the AOR, so we sell it dirt cheap to state and local governments.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T07:47:57-07:00","id":181951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=181900#post181900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is somewhat misleading. Only the records which were held by the LEC were refused under the ACLU's request. The swat policies and procedures executed by the police departments (who actually administer the SWAT teams) were turned over in compliance with the law. The only records refused were those that have to do with administering the tests and qualifications exams, which is what the LEC runs as a not for profit contractor signed on by the local government.<br /> <br /> Really, the local governments just need to have the police departments run those directly.<br /> <br /> It sounds conspiratorial as hell, but it's actually not. (Not to say there aren't other signs of police militarization... most of which is driven by the DOD needing to shed vehicles we were ordered to purchase and couldn't use outside the AOR, so we sell it dirt cheap to state and local governments.)</div> </div> </div> </div>It is conspiratorial whether it is meant to be or not. They may/more than likely mean it to be above board, but its actual result isn't and in theory could be taken farther than it is. They shouldn't have been allowed to incorporate in the first place. This is a highly insane situation that should have never been.<br /> <br /> It wouldn't be the first time that Police and Private Security worked together. People laugh but the Patrol Special in Kuffs is a real thing. The difference is in the scope of powers and what they are allowed to use compared to how SWAT is allowed to operate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forever and always forward.<br /> Don't ask why that came to mind or that I felt the need to post it.<br /> I see the incorporeally challenged.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/787-sorwen","name":"Sorwen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T08:28:32-07:00","id":181986,"title":"","content":"Why shouldn't they be allowed to incorporate?<br /> <br /> I mean you basically just said the police should not be allowed to have a union (and I am not stating whether or not I agree... I'm asking your legal opinion on why they shouldn't be allowed to incorporate).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T09:00:18-07:00","id":182018,"title":"","content":"You kind of answer your own question in part. Unions are not Corporations and are governed under different laws. While a step down it would be closer if you said Blackwater was given police jurisdiction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forever and always forward.<br /> Don't ask why that came to mind or that I felt the need to post it.<br /> I see the incorporeally challenged.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/787-sorwen","name":"Sorwen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T09:25:48-07:00","id":182046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=181986#post181986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why shouldn't they be allowed to incorporate?<br /> <br /> I mean you basically just said the police should not be allowed to have a union (and I am not stating whether or not I agree... I'm asking your legal opinion on why they shouldn't be allowed to incorporate).</div> </div> </div> </div>My opinion/concerns are first and foremost lack of transparency. Even if the material inaccessible is just tests and qualifications, that is a significant relevant chunk of data when investigating such questions as the metal fitness or capacity of a police officer, be it by an internal investigation, or an externally driven one.<br /> <br /> My secondary concern is the precedent it is setting in that a privately held not for profit organization now seems to have the authority to own and deploy military spec hardware on US soil. Granted, right now every individual in the organization is a serving police officer, but what happens when the organization starts needing full time employees and employs the Ex-police to maintain the equipment and/or coordinate activities? What happens when it becomes standard practice that the organization is the holder of data for SWAT actions (this makes logical sense)? Where do the rights to arm up come from/end? Where does the right to keep information private come into that picture? What happens as you start to get organizations that expand to cover more than one function/goal and retain the right to have military grade weapons for use in line with the structure and goals of that organization? <br /> <br /> Ultimately I guess you can say my concern is that it opens the door to privately held (not for profit) organizations getting the rights to be heavily armed for the public good, without the public accountability. There's all kinds of nightmare scenarios one can weave around that but that is the heart of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:10:04-07:00","id":182079,"title":"","content":"If I'm a high ranking member of one of those private organizations, and I decide that driving into a Massachusetts town that I'll park wherever I damn well please, I'll remind any police officer that might take issue with my parking spot, that it might not be in his/her best interest to cite me for illegal parking.<br /> <br /> Quite frankly, a private organization has no business whatsoever in enforcing public law. Those organization that do, are open to public scrutiny for the sake of accountability. Anything else is likely to be seen as unconstitutional. (I'm betting that the ACLU will win this case against NEMLEC)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:39:29-07:00","id":182096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorwen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182018#post182018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You kind of answer your own question in part. Unions are not Corporations and are governed under different laws. While a step down it would be closer if you said Blackwater was given police jurisdiction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Umm... yes they are. They are 501(c)5, for the most part, which is a type of Corporation. They are a corporate entity. So... no, my question hasn't been answered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-27-2014, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:41:25-07:00","id":182097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182079#post182079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I'm a high ranking member of one of those private organizations, and I decide that driving into a Massachusetts town that I'll park wherever I damn well please, I'll remind any police officer that might take issue with my parking spot, that it might not be in his/her best interest to cite me for illegal parking.</div> </div> </div> </div> Same goes for the governor or any member of the state legislator in that construct.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Quite frankly, a private organization has no business whatsoever in enforcing public law. Those organization that do, are open to public scrutiny for the sake of accountability. Anything else is likely to be seen as unconstitutional. (I'm betting that the ACLU will win this case against NEMLEC) </div> </div> </div>Look closer. They weren't enforcing laws, they're administering tests and tracking test records on behalf of the state. Law enforcement is what's known as an inherently governmental function and cannot be dolled out to private contracted firms. But this isn't law enforcement, it's related to law enforcement, but not the same thing. I'm betting ACLU won't win this argument in this manner.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:46:52-07:00","id":182100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182046#post182046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My opinion/concerns are first and foremost lack of transparency. Even if the material inaccessible is just tests and qualifications, that is a significant relevant chunk of data when investigating such questions as the metal fitness or capacity of a police officer, be it by an internal investigation, or an externally driven one.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm with you on that one... but that bespeaks the problem in governmental contracting itself: data rights. That's a much different concern and much less conspiratorial.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My secondary concern is the precedent it is setting in that a privately held not for profit organization now seems to have the authority to own and deploy military spec hardware on US soil. Granted, right now every individual in the organization is a serving police officer, but what happens when the organization starts needing full time employees and employs the Ex-police to maintain the equipment and/or coordinate activities? What happens when it becomes standard practice that the organization is the holder of data for SWAT actions (this makes logical sense)? Where do the rights to arm up come from/end? Where does the right to keep information private come into that picture? What happens as you start to get organizations that expand to cover more than one function/goal and retain the right to have military grade weapons for use in line with the structure and goals of that organization? </div> </div> </div>What the article missed on this is that those units which NEMLEC pays for and trains are still under the authority and jurisdiction of the police and those entities are still held in the public trust. Records pertaining to those investigations cited have been turned over in compliance with the law (note the last part... in compliance with the law that should tell you the law is the real issue).<br /> <br /> Now, on the issue police militarization... that's a separate and important issue. That's where we start getting into when does it cease to be a police function and become a Title 10 military function. That's a debate the country desperately needs to have (and quickly).<br /> <br /> EDIT: I will admit I'm out of my litigation trickster element when we cross into police functions... so I'm having to intuit a lot of this from general knowledge and case law. Title 10 is really where I'm more knowledgeable about...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ultimately I guess you can say my concern is that it opens the door to privately held (not for profit) organizations getting the rights to be heavily armed for the public good, without the public accountability. There's all kinds of nightmare scenarios one can weave around that but that is the heart of it. </div> </div> </div> Amen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-27-2014, 01:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:52:30-07:00","id":182104,"title":"","content":"Are you claiming that this <i>&quot;Massachusetts SWAT teams claim they\u2019re private corporations&quot;</i> isn't correct at all?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:54:36-07:00","id":182105,"title":"","content":"I'm claiming it's a half truth that distorts what the LEC claimed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T11:10:23-07:00","id":182110,"title":"","content":"If you want to have a fiction example of where this kind of privatisation might lead any dystopian Scifi novel has plenty of examples and closer to home so to speak the Caldari state in EvE-online is run by mega corps which all have their own private armies policing the corporations assets and interests.<br /> <br /> And there's already dozens of private armies working for the oil &amp; mining companies to protect their assets at the third world were the government is weak or corrupt enough to allow it such as Tsad, Mali or Zaire.<br /> <br /> USA isn't weak or corrupted and should have no need for privately run armies and mercenaries let alone private police. There's a good reason why the contract with Pinkerton Agency was abolished at early 1900's and FBI was formed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1700-blue-fox","name":"Blue Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T12:17:13-07:00","id":182145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blue Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182110#post182110\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And there's already dozens of private armies working for the oil &amp; mining companies to protect their assets at the third world were the government is weak or corrupt enough to allow it such as Tsad, Mali or Zaire.</div> </div> </div> </div>Minor Quibble. Most of the major Oil and Gas companies tend to arrange contracts with the larger organizations such as Academi (Formerly Xe, formerly Blackwater) rather than support a wide range of mercenary groups.<br /> <br /> And honestly it's not just the energy companies, take a look at Chiquita and the funds they paid to terrorist groups to cooperate with them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But related to something Nobody mentioned, what with the police armed up with military hardware as a standard thing these days, I got curious and did a spot of research. See, apart from a slight spike back up in the last few years (economic woes tend to cause that) violent crime has been on a steady decline for the last 2 generations. Reporting is WAY up, but violence is down. <br /> <br /> But the ultimate gauge of serious issue is death, so I looked to police deaths in the line of duty. And there are good and bad years, but the thing is the US has had between 100-200 deaths per year in the line of duty for the last CENTURY. <br /> <br /> So while the population has increased 4 times, officer deaths have remained the same. <a href=\"http://www2.fbi.gov/ucr/cius2009/data/table_74.html\" target=\"_blank\">Currently about 0.3% of the population at large</a> is a law enforcement officer one one variety or another (prison, federal, state, local), and unfortunately I can't find statistics for the country going back more than a decade or two on that front, but the simple population grown vs decreasing deaths speak to it becoming it a safer job. But let's put this into some standard numbers, we have roughly 1,000,000 police in the US and 300 million people, with 100-200 deaths per year in the force, giving us 10-20 deaths per hundred thousand. The reason for this rather odd number is standardization. For comparison, independent contractors, the realm that usually falls to the construction workers of various sorts and <a href=\"http://www.cultureofsafety.com/2013/01/your-odds-of-dying-at-work/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancers in general</a> have a number of 12.7 per 100k. For comparison <a href=\"http://apps.usfa.fema.gov/firefighter-fatalities/fatalityData/statistics\" target=\"_blank\">Firefighters </a>have a death rate of 2.33, and the average is in 3.5 range.<br /> <br /> So even before the police started getting military hardware things were steadily getting better, implying at least that it's not the presence of assault weapons and body armor that render police safer.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T12:53:17-07:00","id":182161,"title":"","content":"Probably what needs to happen in the US is that we stop the military from selling its excess hardware to local law enforcement. However, this creates a problem of then what do we do with the excess hardware? That's a lot of shit to go to waste.<br /> <br /> Another facet to consider: the military does not produce this hardware. Contractors do... who can then, often, turn around and sell it to someone else because we don't buy the data rights and the tech data packages as part of our standard fielding &amp; production contractrs. Turns out TDPs are very expensive and a contractor does not like to give up his bread and butter, so they make it cost prohibitive for the military to buy it outright.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T14:10:53-07:00","id":182221,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182161#post182161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Probably what needs to happen in the US is that we stop the military from selling its excess hardware to local law enforcement. However, this creates a problem of then what do we do with the excess hardware? That's a lot of shit to go to waste.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well the traditional method is to sell/gift the stuff to other countries, preferably with nice and expensive maintenance contracts.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem is also of supply, demand, storage, and changing tech. <br /> <br /> The M1 Abrams tank is a good example of this. The factory that manufactures them is exceeding demand in the US. They are quite literally rolling off the line and into storage against the assumption of another order in 3 years or so. The argument was raised, why not shut it down then? The problem is that it costs more to do that. To shut the line down and fire all the people is fine and will save money for at least the next 3 years. The problem being that to fire up the line again should an order come in 3 years hence, to find people able to use the equipment and meet the requirements and certifications, will cost *more* than just running the plant pointlessly for 3 years.<br /> <br /> Nature of manufacturing, we have reached a point with technology and manufacturing that we have a minimum volume produced.<br /> <br /> Demand, well that's a straight forward one. Unsurprisingly when you're not active horrific ground wars, equipment isn't being destroyed and doesn't need to be replaced. This is doubly true when you're going through force size reductions.<br /> <br /> Storage... surprisingly you need some fairly specific conditions to successfully store goods for long periods of time, which are difficult and expensive to set up and maintain. So that's a headache right there.<br /> <br /> Finally the big one, changing tech. Even if we had say a huge old salt mine that was re-purposed to military storage and the option was chosen to simply store all the extra guns, tanks, planes and weapons against future need in future wars... it wouldn't matter. technology continually updates and military hardware is one of those big drivers. So dumping a bunch of extra equipment into storage, even if that storage were to exist, isn't actually going to be terribly helpful for the future because it'll be vastly outdated by the time it's needed.<br /> <br /> <br /> So we rapidly fall into a situation where because of the need to keep up on the newest hardware and have minimum manufacturing volumes, there is going to be a large volume of surplus equipment, be it a generation old or just not in current demand.<br /> <br /> Spinning off from that thought, there are a lot of amusing things from the end of WWII when a more significant equipment glut existed, in some cases (such as the artificial deep reef in the pacific) it was simply tossed overboard, in some cases it was just distributed via returning troops (my grandfather was discharged with his side arm, rifle and main weapon which would be disturbing if his main weapon wasn't a PIAT gun), but frankly that solution can't really be applied now. Besides the increasing gun control measures, dumping several hundred thousand surplus military grade weapons on the general market would be a disaster.<br /> <br /> <br /> But to jump back into the problem, odd as it sounds technology *might* have a solution of sorts for the minimum manufacturing volume issue... If the work force is replaced exclusively with robotic systems, those systems are a lot easier to re-purpose and change around as needed. Unfortunately that's not likely to happen because of the &quot;American Jobs&quot; angle.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T14:26:15-07:00","id":182231,"title":"","content":"Your reasoning is far more spot on than you might even realize... unless you work in the space and haven't told me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The Foreign Military Sales guys in the row next to me appreciated that someone in the civilian world knows what the hell they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T15:11:15-07:00","id":182250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/181472-massachusetts-swat-teams-claim-they\u2019re-private-corporations?p=182231#post182231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Your reasoning is far more spot on than you might even realize... unless you work in the space and haven't told me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The Foreign Military Sales guys in the row next to me appreciated that someone in the civilian world knows what the hell they do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh, it's something that I keep sort of an eye on because, well frankly it has significant global impact. It is hardly out of line to say the US is the major arms manufacturer to the world, and that has impact both in the US and abroad. <br /> <br /> ... and the tactic of giving away something highly valued for cheap to secure someone into a long-term high margin deal is one of the bedrocks of the software industry which I currently work in and a fairly common tactic in the oil &amp; gas industries that I used to work in.<br /> <br /> <br /> And honestly the manufacturing supply vs demand aspects are something that have been a growing issue in all production, not just in ones with military applicability. Currently much of the global market place is absorbing the problems produced by throwing our readily available cheap shipping at it, and the fact that most classically luxury items (mostly electronics and personal electronics) that are being transported have a very finite lifespan due to technology changes... Or as an example, if I make a few hundred thousand smart phones that don't sell, I can cheaply ship them to Africa or other secondary markets to be sold off at a slight profit, or else due to the fact that they would only be salable for a year or two no matter what, I just give them away.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately having the ability to get rid of minimum lot sizes requires the ability to very rapidly change around manufacturing processes. And humans simply can't hack the speed of doing that. It takes time for us to learn and adapt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T16:59:38-07:00","id":182377,"title":"","content":"From the OP: <i>Some of these LECs have also apparently incorporated as 501(c)(3) organizations. And it\u2019s here that we run into problems. According to the ACLU, the LECs are claiming that the 501(c)(3) status means that they\u2019re private corporations, not government agencies. And therefore, they say they\u2019re immune from open records requests. Let\u2019s be clear. These agencies oversee police activities. They employ cops who carry guns, wear badges, collect paychecks provided by taxpayers and have the power to detain, arrest, injure and kill. They operate SWAT teams, which conduct raids on private residences. And yet they say that because they\u2019ve incorporated, they\u2019re immune to Massachusetts open records laws. The state\u2019s residents aren\u2019t permitted to know how often the SWAT teams are used, what they\u2019re used for, what sort of training they get or who they\u2019re primarily used against.</i>................Seems to me that should be an issue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T19:55:27-07:00","id":182451,"title":"","content":"Except the article is wrong. LECs do not &quot;oversee police activities.&quot; Nor were all the records the article implies were denied... denied. What the article is complaining about is that some of the records held by the LEC were denied. Is that still worrisome? Yes. Is it unusual? No.<br /> <br /> Does being a 501(c)(3) render you immune to open government laws? Not actually... if the records were records pertaining to law enforcement activities (as opposed to equipping, training, and procurement, and general non-enforcement operations) then they wouldn't be required to release them. My point is that the article has basically vastly oversimplified what's going on to give you the reader's digest version of what happened with no thought or understanding of the underlying issues.<br /> <br /> What usually happens in that case is you think your lawgivers fix the issue when people bitch about it, but they really just obfuscate the matter for themselves. Because you don't understand what they're doing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T20:44:01-07:00","id":182499,"title":"","content":"How do you know the article is wrong?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T20:45:59-07:00","id":182500,"title":"","content":"Because you can google the court cases in question and the related documents are all in the public record. Have fun reading about 700 pages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T21:00:39-07:00","id":182512,"title":"","content":"Oh, so you've already read it then and can show me the relevant source? From Techdirt; <a href=\"https://www.techdirt.com/articles/20140627/09052427701/mass-swat-teams-claim-theyre-private-corporations-to-get-out-transparency-requests.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">Massachusetts SWAT Teams Claim They're Private Corporations To Get Out Of Transparency Requests</a> Lets see now...BoingBoing; <a href=\"http://boingboing.net/2014/06/27/swat-teams-claim-to-be-private.html\" target=\"_blank\">SWAT teams claim to be private mercenaries, immune to open records laws</a>...next up, DailyKos; <a href=\"http://www.dailykos.com/story/2014/06/27/1309949/-Mass-SWAT-Teams-Deny-Records-Request-Claiming-They-Are-Private-Corporations\" target=\"_blank\">Mass. SWAT Teams Deny Records Request Claiming They Are Private 